```java
double calculateShipping(Order order) {
    if (order.getType().equals("STANDARD")) {
        return order.getWeight() * 0.5;
    } else if (order.getType().equals("EXPRESS")) {
        return order.getWeight() * 1.2 + 5.0;
    } else if (order.getType().equals("OVERNIGHT")) {
        return order.getWeight() * 2.0 + 15.0;
    }
    throw new IllegalArgumentException("Unknown type");
}
```

Every new shipping type means another `else if` here, and the method's cyclomatic complexity keeps climbing — a classic candidate for this kind of refactor.

```java
double calculateShipping(Order order) {
    return switch (order.getType()) {
        case "STANDARD" -> standardShipping(order);
        case "EXPRESS" -> expressShipping(order);
        case "OVERNIGHT" -> overnightShipping(order);
        default -> throw new IllegalArgumentException(order.getType());
    };
}
private double standardShipping(Order o) { return o.getWeight() * 0.5; }
private double expressShipping(Order o) { return o.getWeight() * 1.2 + 5.0; }
```

Extracting each branch first, before introducing any pattern, keeps the refactor reversible at every step and makes the next step's diff much smaller to review.

```java
interface ShippingCalculator { double calculate(Order order); }

Map<String, ShippingCalculator> calculators = Map.of(
    "STANDARD", o -> o.getWeight() * 0.5,
    "EXPRESS", o -> o.getWeight() * 1.2 + 5.0,
    "OVERNIGHT", o -> o.getWeight() * 2.0 + 15.0
);

double calculateShipping(Order order) {
    return calculators.get(order.getType()).calculate(order);
}
```

This is [Strategy](strategy-pattern) implemented as a lookup map of lambdas rather than a full class hierarchy — adding a new shipping type is now one new map entry, not a new branch or a new class.
